  "description": "flingy.dat holds the movement data of everything that moves or can move: units and bullets. Each entry, called a flingy, gives the sprite to create, how fast it may go, how it accelerates, when it starts braking and how fast it turns. A unit type picks its flingy with the Graphics field of units.dat, and a weapon picks the flingy of its bullet with the Graphics field of weapons.dat.\n\nThe file is arr\\flingy.dat in the game's MPQ archives. It has 209 entries, one for each flingy, and is 3135 bytes long. It is stored field by field, not entry by entry: first the Sprite of all 209 flingies, then the Top Speed of all 209, and so on, with nothing in between. The offsets below count from the start of the file.\n\nThe layout is the same in every version from 1.04 to 1.17, in both StarCraft and Brood War. StarCraft 1.00 to 1.03 used an older layout with 184 entries (2760 bytes). That older file is still in StarDat.mpq in later versions, but it is never loaded, because the game finds the copy in BrooDat.mpq (or patch_rt.mpq) first. From 1.08 to 1.17, patch_rt.mpq has its own copy. It differs from the one in BrooDat.mpq in two entries: the Dropship's top speed went from 1133 to 1400, and the Valkyrie's top speed from 1450 to 1690 and its acceleration from 48 to 65. Their Halt Distance was left at the old values.\n\nThe game loads the file each time a game starts. StarEdit reads it as well, but only uses the Sprite field. If the file is longer than the layout, the game stops with a file error. A shorter file loads without an error if it ends exactly where a field ends; the missing fields then keep whatever they held before. Out-of-range values are not rejected: the game then reads whatever follows the table that the value points into.\n\nWhen a unit or bullet is created, the game copies the movement fields into it, so a later change to these values only affects new units and bullets. Positions are kept in 1/256 of a pixel, and the movement values use the same scale: a speed of 256 is one pixel per game frame."
}
